fullDescription.text:
Reports methods whose names are too short, too long, or do not follow the specified regular expression pattern. A quick-fix to rename such methods is available only in the editor. Instance methods that override library methods and constructors are ignored by this inspection. Example: 'public static int n() {
    return 0;
  }' This example will be reported if the inspection is enabled for 'static' methods with the default settings where a method name should have a minimum length of 4. Use the list in the Options section to specify the types of methods that should be checked. For each method type, you can specify the minimum length, maximum length, and regular expression for acceptable method names. Specify 0 in a length field to skip the corresponding check. Regular expressions use the standard 'java.util.regex' format. Inspection ID: NewMethodNamingConvention

Reports code that applies '==' or '!=' to a newly allocated object instead of calling 'equals()'. The references to newly allocated objects cannot point at existing objects, thus the comparison will always evaluate to 'false'. The inspection may also report newly created objects returned from simple methods. Example: 'void test(Object obj) {
    if (new Object() == obj) {...}
  }' After the quick-fix is applied: 'void test(Object obj) {
    if (new Object().equals(obj)) {...}
  }' New in 2018.3 Inspection ID: NewObjectEquality

fullDescription.markdown:
Reports code that applies `==` or `!=` to a newly allocated object instead of calling `equals()`.


The references to newly allocated objects cannot point at existing objects,
thus the comparison will always evaluate to `false`. The inspection may also report newly
created objects returned from simple methods.

**Example:**


      void test(Object obj) {
        if (new Object() == obj) {...}
      }

After the quick-fix is applied:


      void test(Object obj) {
        if (new Object().equals(obj)) {...}
